WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.940
- Watch, she's moving.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.290
- [Wes] Have you ever tried
to roll over a thousand-pound

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.250
unconscious elephant seal?

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.520
- Up up, get her up and over.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.580
Hold her nose really tight.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.270
- I hadn't either.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.503
Take it from me, it's not easy.

00:00:16.774 --> 00:00:18.730
(lively music)

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.200
- I'm Wes Larson, a wildlife biologist

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.460
and lover of the outdoors for
as long as I can remember.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.840
I'm traveling the country to find

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.100
the next generation of conservationists,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.750
the people on the front lines of the fight

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.010
to understand our planet
and protect the animals

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.000
we share it with.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.190
This time around I'm on the West Coast,

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.060
just North of Monterey Bay.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.420
I'm meeting up with an
elephant seal research team

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.860
at The University of
California Santa Cruz.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:47.693
Hey.

00:00:47.693 --> 00:00:48.678
- Hey, good morning.

00:00:48.678 --> 00:00:49.511
- Good morning.

00:00:49.511 --> 00:00:50.344
- Nice to see you.

00:00:50.344 --> 00:00:51.177
We got a busy day ahead,

00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:52.120
so if you could grab one of these jackets

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.230
and then we will head up to the beach.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.280
- [Wes] Rachel Holser has
been studying elephant seals

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.350
at the Costa lab for four years

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.080
and believes tracking elephant seals

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.883
could hold the key to
a deeper understanding

00:01:01.883 --> 00:01:03.543
of the ocean as a whole.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.960
Our mission this morning is to attach

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.820
some high-tech tracking
tags to an elephant seal,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.500
but not just any seal will do.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.760
- We are a little bit particular

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.690
about the animals we
put satellite tags on,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.540
so an adult female over 4 years old

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.290
and under 13 years old,
hasn't been tracked before,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.200
and has been on the
beach for nearly a month.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.860
- In short they are looking for a seal

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.580
that has had her pup and is ready

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.490
to head back out into the sea.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.940
Rachel tells me that there
are seven promising candidates

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.900
on the beach today, which
sounds like a lot to me,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.150
but then I see the colony
for the first time.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.710
We just kinda rounded this hill

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.210
and suddenly all these seals
are spread out in front of us.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.730
One thing that's super surprising

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.800
is the amount of
vocalization that's going on,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.198
all the seals kind of talking
to each other out here.

00:01:50.198 --> 00:01:53.270
(seals making sounds)

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.810
It's a lot to take in.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.000
The seals are big, loud, and
absolutely cover the beach.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.190
- Take binos, take a
re-site data book, for sure,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.430
and yeah, we'll see what we can find.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:05.720
- The researchers are fanning out.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.010
They're just going seal to seal

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.130
trying to find the particular female

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.870
that they're trying to tag today.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.480
- [Wes] The couple thousand
seals on the beach.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:14.550
It's like looking for a needle

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.280
in a very noisy, potentially
dangerous hay stack,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.670
but it's worth the risk.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.210
It turns out, if you wanna study

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.140
the farthest reaches of the ocean,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.740
elephant seals are natural
deep-sea explorers.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.300
- They go way out into the
middle of the North Pacific,

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.513
and they dive far deeper than
anybody thought was possible.

00:02:32.386 --> 00:02:34.820
- [Wes] And eventually,
Rachel spots the perfect seal.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.750
- If you're looking straight down,

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.260
there's two animals back to back.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.170
One has a pup nursing on her right side.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.230
I think that that is her.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.100
We had a good look at her.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.900
So we're estimating her
mass at about 340 kilograms.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.300
So we're gonna pull up a
3.4 cc dose of Telazol.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.880
- And so you physically just go up

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.100
and put the needle in 'em.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:54.933
- Yep.

00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:57.510
That is how this goes.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.480
- That's a big needle.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.150
That's to get through all that blubber.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.373
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.210
- There's a big male over there.

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.990
It's actually the alpha
male of this colony,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.560
so we're kinda just waiting for them

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.710
to get positioned in a way

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.970
to where the researchers
can go in, sedate the seal,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.640
and then attach their tag.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.250
This is the most dangerous
part of the research.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.920
Rachel has to inject the sedative by hand,

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.773
and elephant seals can be unpredictable.

00:03:24.772 --> 00:03:25.640
- They are very dangerous animals.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.810
I know they look pretty lazy and sleepy

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.820
if they're on a beach, but they can move

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.760
surprisingly quickly and
react surprisingly quickly.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.994
And the big guys, you know,
the males, are almost two tons,

00:03:35.994 --> 00:03:38.723
and they can run as fast
as a person can run.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.113
We spend years being trained
to work with them properly.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.590
- Did you get the seal stuck?

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.467
- Yes, we did.

00:03:58.052 --> 00:04:00.310
(lively music)

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:03.610
- Oh oh, just keep a
light hand on her nose

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.690
just in case she pulls out again.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.380
- There's a bunch of different
things going on right now.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.090
It's pretty impressive.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.360
Theresa's taking these
ultrasound measurements

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.050
with the blubber.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.730
These two collaborators
are putting on the epoxy

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.680
for this tag that we're attaching

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.770
to get that oceanographic data.

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:24.653
And then on top we're
also attaching a VHF tag.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.070
Rachel's kinda organizing everyone

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.820
and directing everyone
to do what they're doing.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.503
- Grab another popsicle stick.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.160
- [Wes] Meanwhile, as always,
we have all these other seals,

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.140
so we kind of always have
to be keeping an eye on

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.788
to make sure that no one's coming up

00:04:36.788 --> 00:04:40.313
and aggressively kind of
challenging the group.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.170
- Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.103
Thank you, Patrick.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.060
We've been monitoring the
population at Año Nuevo

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.620
for between three and four decades now.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.820
But if the elephant
seals are not doing well

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.485
that means that something
is really going sideways

00:04:57.485 --> 00:04:59.350
down in the food web.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.883
- We got another seal kind
of creeping back here.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.410
Alright, so yeah, one of
the really cool things

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:09.160
about these tags is
they're actually recording

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.790
oceanographic information with them.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.760
All sorts of things that
they would normally record

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.290
with like a really
expensive research vessel,

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.203
they're using these seals
kinda as that research vessel.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.520
And elephant seals make
exceptionally good research vessels.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:23.830
After they leave the colony

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.810
they swim out into the
Pacific for thousands of miles

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.110
diving over a mile below the surface.

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.850
And all the while the tags
will be collecting data.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.290
What you end up with is a
three-dimensional profile

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.830
of the ocean.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.523
That is, if all goes as planned.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.660
It's just so crazy to
have, like, this happening

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.620
20 feet away, 10 feet away,
as we're working on this seal.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.793
At times it reminded me of being in an ER.

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:49.890
- [Woman Offscreen] Half cc kit.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.200
- And others, it was like
being on another planet

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.577
surrounded by hostile aliens.

00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:56.994
(elephant seal growling)

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:58.850
One thing that's really interesting

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.990
is this kind of like matador
act that they have going on.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:02.980
It's just a tarp.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.341
I mean if the thing wanted to
come through they totally can.

00:06:05.341 --> 00:06:06.370
And so at all points we have to be ready

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.800
to kind of back off and run away

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.890
if one of these seals kind of

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.143
crosses that threshold of safety.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.540
Through the chaos the team gets it done,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.270
deploying three different tags

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.220
and giving the seal a full checkup.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:22.010
- Alright, you guys can
go ahead and roll her

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.103
on Theresa's count.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:25.913
OK, go ahead.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:31.143
Good, breath.

00:06:31.143 --> 00:06:31.976
Go ahead and pull it.

00:06:31.976 --> 00:06:33.540
- So far, so good.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.070
With the science complete
there's one important step left:

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.030
waiting for our seal to wake up.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.480
- We'll stay nearby, so that if a male

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.390
decides to try and mate
with her, we can prevent it.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.630
We're fully responsible for
the well-being of the animal

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:52.190
until she is capable of
taking care of herself.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.190
So, it looks like our
female's doing really well.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.450
She's recovering nicely.

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.090
She's moved back into the group.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.580
And hopefully, in about three days,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.380
she'll head off to sea and
give us lots of awesome data.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.850
- [Wes] Looking out at the
colony it's hard to imagine

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.973
that a hundred years ago elephant
seals nearly disappeared.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.399
- [Rachel] They did almost go extinct

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:16.050
back in the early 1900s.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.220
Once the seal was protected and hunting

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.284
was no longer permitted, they rebounded

00:07:20.284 --> 00:07:23.100
and they have been spreading ever since.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:24.370
- [Wes] And now you guys are using them

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.090
in all these kind of new novel ways

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.550
to learn more about the ocean,
to protect other species.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.950
- [Rachel] Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.270
- I found that conservation success

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.770
can sometimes be as simple
as changing perceptions.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:38.970
Rachel and her team have shown

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.110
that an animal that appears
to just lie around on a beach

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.960
is actually an incredible
traveler and athlete.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.300
And by harnessing their power

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.900
we are able to gain a deeper
understanding of the ocean.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.790
It makes me wonder how
else we could be working

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:54.490
with wildlife to protect the
planet that we all share.

